<sn><md>### Key Terminology &amp; Notation

| Concept | Description / Rule | Examples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Set Designation** | Represented using uppercase letters ($A, B, C, \dots, X, Y, Z$). | $A, B$ |
| **Elements** | The individual objects within a set, usually denoted using lowercase letters ($a, b, c, \dots, x, y, z$). | $a, b, c$ |
| **Enclosure Symbol** | Elements are enclosed within curly braces `{ }`. | $\{a, b, c\}$ |
| **Membership Symbol ($\in$)** | Indicates that an item belongs to a set. Written as $x \in A$ and read as *"x is an element of A"* or *"x belongs to A"*. | If $B = \{m, n\}$, then $m \in B$ and $n \in B$. |

[diagram](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Math/19.Sets/resources/set-notation-diagram.jpeg)</md></sn><sn><md>#### Examples of Sets

* **Rivers:** $\{\text{Tista}, \text{Meghna}, \text{Jamuna}, \text{Brahmaputra}\}$
* **First two even natural numbers:** $\{2, 4\}$
* **Factors of 6:** $\{1, 2, 3, 6\}$
* **General collections:** First five English alphabets, countries of Asia, natural numbers.</md></sn>